<header><p class="enc-kicker">Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Arawakan languages</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Arawakan languages are a family of languages of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-america">South America</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a>, spoken from the Andes and Amazonia to the Antilles. Before the Spanish conquest they included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taino">Taíno</a> in the Greater Antilles and the Island Carib language of the Lesser Antilles, ancestor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/garifuna">Garifuna</a>. Living members include Wayuu, Asháninka and Garifuna in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-america">Central America</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/arawakan-languages.jpg" alt="Arawakan languages" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Davius · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Arawak-Languages.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
